For a sinusoidal input, the steady-state output is obtained by evaluating the transfer function at \[s=j\omega.\] Given, \[H(s)=\frac{1}{s(s+4)},\] and the input is \[\cos(3t),\] so \[\omega=3\ \text{rad/s}.\] Hence, \[H(j3)=\frac{1}{(j3)(4+j3)}.\] The output amplitude is \[A=\left|H(j3)\right|
=\frac{1}{|j3|\,|4+j3|}
=\frac{1}{3\times\sqrt{4^2+3^2}}
=\frac{1}{3\times5}
=\frac{1}{15}.\] Also, \[\angle H(j3)
=-\left(90^\circ+\tan^{-1}\frac{3}{4}\right)
=-126.87^\circ.\] Since \[\cos(3t-126.87^\circ)
=\sin(3t-36.87^\circ),\] the output can be written in the form \[A\sin(3t+\alpha),\] where only the amplitude \(A\) is required. \[\boxed{\text{(b) }\dfrac{1}{15}}\]

For the LTI system, \[h(t)=e^{-5t}u(t),\] the transfer function is \[H(s)=\frac{1}{s+5}.\] The output is \[y(t)=e^{-3t}u(t)-e^{-5t}u(t),\] whose Laplace transform is \[Y(s)=\frac{1}{s+3}-\frac{1}{s+5}
=\frac{2}{(s+3)(s+5)}.\] Since \[Y(s)=H(s)X(s),\] we get \[X(s)=\frac{Y(s)}{H(s)}
=\frac{2(s+5)}{(s+3)(s+5)}
=\frac{2}{s+3}.\] Taking the inverse Laplace transform, \[x(t)=2e^{-3t}u(t).\] \[\boxed{\text{(b) }2e^{-3t}u(t)}\]

